board_guidance.expect_to_see:
For every Board item, the package should give the following.
(1) The grid row of the submitted orders and the matching row with that item at hold, column by column S1-S6, with the item value stated. Example: 'UltraFan NB: hold + commit to JV' minus 'UltraFan NB: hold' = 0.00 / 0.00 / 0.00 / +2.28 / 0.00 / 0.00. Any cancel or widebody row that was considered should also be shown against hold, so the record carries it.
(2) The CEO's expected scenario and `predictions` for 2035:
- whether NGSA keeps code 7 or moves to code 6 (S2);
- whether Boeing launches fps, which variant, and which code (its record says code 3 by default, or code 7 if P&W and RR are ready by EIS);
- P&W's Joint Venture stance (it says 'not today', and will consider once a partner's commitment is on the record);
- any 787 or A350 Re-engine.
(3) From McCabe:
- her co-signature on any `commit`;
- her column weights (we tested an illustrative S1 0.55 / S2 0.10 / S3 0.05 / S4 0.10 / S5 0.12 / S6 0.08, uniform weights and an S2-heavy set, and no vote changes; inference);
- each item's worst column against its bill;
- capex a year in GBP for the remaining Solo spend (about $1.14B a year to 2037), and how much a 2035 Joint Venture formation would save ($8.0B to $5.7B);
- the standing-commitment case: what a P&W commit in 2045 would do once both solo engines are ready, and the bill it could carry.
(4) From Watson:
- UltraFan NB ready in 2037 against NGSA EIS 2037 (zero margin), and progress against his internal gates: partner-shop agreements signed in the trigger year, capacity qualified by 2036, support live before the first EIS;
- if the Joint Venture is committed, its engine's ready year when formed in 2035: the folded Solo makes it 2037 or earlier, against 2041 (formation + 6) without the fold. He should check this against NGSA 2037 and any fps EIS (7-year 2042, 10-year 2045), and state whether he sees any compressed maturity.
(5) Both veto checks, every red-line flag struck or rebutted in `rationale`, and `overrides` empty.
(6) `premium_b`. We expect 0 if only the Joint Venture commit or hold is submitted. Any widebody shortfall must be declared with its reason, and it would exceed the caps.
(7) A public statement that:
- gives no UltraFan entry into service before 2037 and no Joint Venture date;
- says nothing on NGSA's or fps dates;
- keeps support in place before EIS and durability proven before EIS;
- if the Joint Venture is committed, replaces 'we commit when our partner does' with a plain statement that we have committed, on terms that credit our technology.
(8) A plain line on the objectives:
- The $4B bounded-risk line can no longer be met on any row (minimum $5.7B). Today's figure is $8.0B; it narrows to $5.7B only if the Joint Venture forms.
- Widebody share is 55.2% in 2042-2050, at or above 50%.
- Strain stays at $0 only while no widebody programme is on the record. From 2037 the Solo is ready and can no longer be cancelled, so any later widebody programme carries the $5B for good.

would_veto:
`jv_with_pw: commit` in any of three cases:
- McCabe's co-signature is withheld or absent (governance ground);
- a binding Civil veto from Watson still stands against the final orders, for example compressed maturity, a disclosed Joint Venture ready or EIS date earlier than the folded Solo's 2037 or than formation + 6, or no support plan before EIS (safety and maturity ground);
- it is submitted with an override.
Acceptable alternative: `hold`.

recommendations.text:
Keep the public statement this disciplined:
- UltraFan entry into service no earlier than 2037, and no Joint Venture date;
- nothing on NGSA's or the fps dates;
- support and MRO through partner shops in place before entry into service, and durability proven before it;
- standard terms only;
- no widebody UltraFan until a Re-engine is on the table.
Show the reasonable worst case of every item against its own bill, as last round's package did.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (public, not priced):
- an answer to Boeing's engine-sourcing invitation, on standard terms, with durability proven before entry into service and no dates;
- the partner-shop support plan;
